* START-DESCRIPTION:
* MODIFY.ACCOUNT account action {action ...}      every action except ADD and DELETE, any number in one command (25 Oct 26)
* MODIFY.ACCOUNT account <ADD/DELETE> user.name
* MODIFY.ACCOUNT account SSH               - ssh only
* MODIFY.ACCOUNT account API               - the API only
* MODIFY.ACCOUNT account BOTH              - ssh and the API
* MODIFY.ACCOUNT account NONE              - neither
* MODIFY.ACCOUNT account SH-ON | SH-OFF    - the SH verb and "!"
* MODIFY.ACCOUNT account OS-ON | OS-OFF    - OS.EXECUTE and the screen editors
* MODIFY.ACCOUNT account AI-ON | AI-OFF    - membership of the Windows group sdaiusers, the right to use the AI (13 Nov 26)
* MODIFY.ACCOUNT account SSH-KEY-ON | SSH-KEY-OFF - membership of the Windows group sdsshnokey, key sign-in over ssh (17 Nov 26)
* MODIFY.ACCOUNT account SUSPENDED | UNSUSPENDED
*
* 17 Nov 26 Windows port - from Linux 1557e270 (S.62 LC3).  SSH-KEY-ON / SSH-KEY-OFF take the account's Windows user out of the Windows group sdsshnokey or put it
*   in.  sshd offers a member of the group no key sign-in (allow-ssh-groups.ps1's arm, PubkeyAuthentication no and nothing else): it signs in with its password, if
*   its ssh gate is on.  create.account puts every Windows user it MAKES in the group, so key sign-in is off as delivered for those accounts; SSH-KEY-ON is the
*   administrator's loosening, account by account, and SSH-KEY-OFF takes it back.  A user account only, and only one whose Windows user carries SD's "SD account"
*   description (!is_sd_user): an attached user - the installing user, a sign-in that was there before SD - is refused (15170), because the keys it set up outside
*   SD are its own.  The line printed is "Account %1 may sign in over ssh with a key: yes|no", read back from the group.  It takes effect at the next ssh
*   connection: sshd reads the groups each time.  An account that existed before this release is not in the group, and an upgrade does not put it there.
*
* 13 Nov 26 Windows port - from Linux S.69 step 2 (23 Oct 26), RELEASE_1.1 246.  AI-ON / AI-OFF add or remove the account's person from the Windows group sdaiusers.
*   A person in it may use the AI provider the administrator chose (set.ai, the same provider and model for every account) through the helper sd-ai; a person
*   outside it is refused by the helper (nogate).  Off for a new account.  The line printed is "Account %1 has ai yes|no" (12611), read back from the group.  It
*   takes effect at the person's next request: the helper reads the group each time (NetLocalGroupGetMembers), with no sign-out.
*
* 18 Sep 26 Windows port - RELEASE_1.1 64.  THE TIERS ARE GONE, AND THESE ARE
*   THE TWO STATE FORMS THAT REMAIN.  SUSPENDED writes SUSPENDED to
*   ACC$SUSPENDED (field 5) and UNSUSPENDED clears it; nothing else in the
*   account moves either way - no Windows membership, no os.users record, no
*   VOC change, because every account's VOC is the whole of NEWVOC whatever the
*   field says.
*
* SUSPENDED DENIES ACCESS AND CHANGES NOTHING ELSE.  No Windows group
*   membership moves and the VOC is left exactly as it is, which is what makes
*   lifting one free.  Three doors refuse a suspended account: LOGIN, CPROC's
*   logto.authorised and APISRVR's vb.account.
*
* THE KEYWORD IS ABSOLUTE.  It says what the account's remote access is, and
* whatever it does not name is withdrawn - so API takes ssh away.  The same
* four words are CREATE.ACCOUNT's, so access is said one way whether an account
* is being made or changed.
*
* A GROUP ACCOUNT IS REFUSED (10087).  A group account has no Windows user to
* put in a group; it is reached with LOGTO.
*
* THE ADMINISTRATOR REFUSALS ARE GONE, 18 Sep 26 - RELEASE_1.1 64.  The tiers
* are removed, so a Windows administrator's SD account is an ordinary account
* and sets its routes and its OS.USERS fields like any other; the only limit
* on what it can do is the one Windows imposes.  The 21 Aug / 27 Aug rules and
* the 18 Sep 58/62 posture this description used to record died with the tier
* that made the account an administrator.
*
* THE FOUR OS FORMS REFUSE A GROUP ACCOUNT (10105).  They are four switches
* over two fields rather than four names for one state, so SH-OFF leaves
* OS.EXECUTE alone.
*
* THE TWO REMOTE ROUTES, AND THERE IS NO THIRD.  The console and Remote Desktop
* are denied to every account SD creates - sdsshonly, applied by CREATE.ACCOUNT
* and enforced by gplbld/deny-logon.ps1 - and no verb here lifts that.  An
* administrator reaches the keyboard because Windows already calls them one,
* not because SD granted it.
*
* EACH IS ONE GROUP MEMBERSHIP AND NOTHING IS WRITTEN TO ACCOUNTS.  sdssh is
* what sshd's AllowGroups names; sdapi is what APISRVR tests after the SCRAM
* proof succeeds.  A mirrored field could only ever disagree with the thing
* that actually decides - the reasoning that deleted ACC$USERS on 15 Aug.
*
* END-DESCRIPTION
*
* START-CODE

$internal
program $modify.account
$catalog $MODIFYA

$include keys.h
$include int$keys.h
$include parser.h
$include err.h
* 
deffun is_grp_member(user,group) calling '!is_grp_member'
deffun os_group(action,group,member) calling '!os_group'
deffun is_group(group) calling '!is_group'
deffun valid_os_name(name) calling '!valid_os_name'
deffun ps_script(script) calling '!ps_script'
deffun is_sd_user(username) calling '!is_sd_user'

   prompt ''

   if not(kernel(K$ADMINISTRATOR, -1)) then
      stop sysmsg(2001)  ;* Command requires administrator privileges
   end     

   parser = "!PARSER"

   @system.return.code = -ER$ARGS     ;* Preset for argument errors
   
   openpath @sdsys:@ds:'accounts' to acc.f else
      @system.return.code = -status()
      stop sysmsg(6000) ;* Cannot open ACCOUNTS file in SDSYS account
   end

   call @parser(PARSER$RESET, 0, @sentence, 0)
   
   call @parser(PARSER$GET.TOKEN, token.type, token, keyword) ;* Verb modify.Account

   * Get next token, account name
   call @parser(PARSER$GET.TOKEN, token.type, acc.name, keyword)
   if token.type = PARSER$END then
      gosub show.usage
      stop
   end
   *
   acc.name = downcase(acc.name)   ;* account names are always lower case (13 Nov 26, RELEASE_1.1 288, as Linux's 13 Sep 26)
* 22 Sep 26 Windows port - RELEASE_1.1 101.  SAY WHY, INSTEAD OF "Account name is
*   invalid".  Owner, 22 Sep 2026: "the modify.account verb should not accept api
*   or ssh commands for the sdsys account - remote access is always off."  The
*   REFUSAL IS UNCHANGED and still comes before the register is read; only the
*   message changes, because 2202 told somebody typing MODIFY.ACCOUNT SDSYS SSH
*   that they had got the NAME wrong, which is the one thing they had right.
*
*   IT COVERS EVERY ACTION, NOT JUST THE TWO HE NAMED, and that is deliberate:
*   the refusal is reached before the action token is parsed, so there is no
*   action to name here, and SDSYS is outside this verb's reach for all of them.
* 19 Nov 26 Windows port - RELEASE_1.1 336 (four-version audit S.62 item 8; Linux 8a300f04's 14189, here 15189 - 14xxx + 1000): 12001 said remote
*   access is "never available" to SDSYS, false since remote.sdsys opens ssh and the API for it (RELEASE_1.1 202, 331).  The message
*   now names the verb that does; the refusal and its place are unchanged.
   if acc.name = 'sdsys' then
      stop sysmsg(15189) ;* modify.account does not change sdsys, the administrator; its remote access is opened and shut with remote.sdsys
   end
* 13 Nov 26 Windows port - RELEASE_1.1 202: SDRADMIN, the remote administration account, is remote.sdsys's alone (a route, a gate or a
*   member here would widen what a network password reaches).
   if acc.name = 'sdradmin' then
      stop sysmsg(12704) ;* SDRADMIN is the remote administration account
   end
   *
   read acc.rec from acc.f, acc.name else      
      stop sysmsg(6003) ;* Account name is invalid
   end
   
   group.name = acc.rec<ACC$GROUP>   ;* get associated group

* 18 Nov 26 Windows port - D3, as Linux (PRE_RELEASE 18 there): THE ACTION WORD IS READ HERE, NOT INSIDE THE is_group BRANCH, because a register
*   change must not depend on the account's Windows group being resolvable.  The group test now guards ADD / DELETE alone (add.delete.arm).
   call @parser(PARSER$GET.TOKEN, token.type, action, keyword)
   if token.type = PARSER$END then
      gosub show.usage
      stop
   end
   typed.first = action
   action = upcase(action)

* 25 Oct 26 Windows port - from Linux 1daee9d (19 Oct 26; owner: "modify.account should be changed to handle more than one gate at a time").
*   A word after the action was read by nobody: MODIFY.ACCOUNT acc VOC-OFF BP-OFF did the first and said nothing of the second (Linux 2287a0a refused
*   the command, 18 Oct).  The words are now ALL carried out, or the whole command is refused BEFORE ANYTHING CHANGES: validate.actions reads every
*   word first (an unknown word 12795, two words that set one access to different states 12796, ADD or DELETE among other words 12719); run.actions
*   then does them in a FIXED order whatever order they were typed, each through do.action (the case block below, unchanged), each reported and
*   audited as it always was, and a run of more than one ends with a line saying which were done (12798) or which were not (12799).  Silence about
*   half of a command is still impossible.  ADD and DELETE take a user name and stand alone: they read it next, and a word after it is refused
*   (further down).  No action at all prints the usage (it fell through to "Action Must Be ...").
   if (action # 'ADD') and (action # 'DELETE') then
      actions = action
      typed.list = typed.first
      loop
         call @parser(PARSER$GET.TOKEN, token.type, token, keyword)
      until token.type = PARSER$END
         actions<-1> = upcase(token)
         typed.list<-1> = token
      repeat
      gosub validate.actions
      gosub run.actions
      stop
   end

   goto add.delete.arm

* 21 Aug 26 Windows port - SSH, API, BOTH and NONE.  Owner's decision, 21 Aug
*   2026, replacing the SSH / NO.SSH / API / NO.API pairs of earlier the same
*   day.  Four actions in the slot ADD and DELETE occupy:
*
*     MODIFY.ACCOUNT <account> SSH    ssh only
*     MODIFY.ACCOUNT <account> API    the API only
*     MODIFY.ACCOUNT <account> BOTH   both
*     MODIFY.ACCOUNT <account> NONE   neither
*
*   THE KEYWORD SAYS WHAT THE ACCESS IS, NOT WHAT TO CHANGE, and that is the
*   whole difference from the pairs it replaces.  "API" used to mean "add the
*   API to whatever it already had"; it now means "the API and nothing else",
*   so it WITHDRAWS ssh.  The same four words are CREATE.ACCOUNT's, so an
*   account's access is said the same way whether it is being made or changed.
*
*   WHICH IS WHY THE MESSAGE STATES THE WHOLE RESULTING STATE rather than what
*   moved.  Someone who types API meaning BOTH has just taken ssh away, and a
*   message reading "may now use the API" would not tell them - it is true and
*   it is the wrong half.  10076-10079 each name both routes.
*
*   They take NO user name - they act on the account's OWN Windows user - so
*   they are handled before the ADD/DELETE test rather than inside it, which
*   would demand one.
*
*   THE PROPERTY IS GROUP MEMBERSHIP AND NOTHING ELSE.  Nothing is written to
*   ACCOUNTS - owner's decision, 20 Aug 2026, kept - so there is no record that
*   could disagree with the group.
*
* 25 Oct 26 Windows port - DO.ACTION: ONE ACTION, called by run.actions once for each word of the command.  action holds the word, in capitals.
* 18 Nov 26 Windows port - D3: Linux's case list, in Linux's order, each case ending in return.  Windows has no SDSYS-ON / SDSYS-OFF (Linux S.103 is not
*   carried: no POSIX groups or modes here, and SDSYS reaches every account by ACL).
do.action:
   begin case
* 25 Oct 26 Windows port - from Linux 858d757 / f047abc (22 Oct 26), S.75: where a session of the account starts (field 16; start.set writes it, blank follows
*   the system default) and whether the person may add their own programs to the home screen (PINS-ON / PINS-OFF, field 18, delivered off).  Any account type.
      case action = 'START-MENU'
         start.want = 'menu'
